<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pavel Tsitsianov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1754">1754</a>–<a href="/years/1806">1806</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pavel Tsitsianov was a Russian general of Georgian descent who commanded Russian forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> from 1802. He stormed Ganja in January 1804, killing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javad-khan">Javad Khan</a>, and forced several khanates to accept Russian protection. In February 1806 he was killed outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> while receiving the city's surrender from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huseyngulu-khan">Huseyngulu Khan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pavel-tsitsianov.jpg" alt="Pavel Tsitsianov" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salvatore Tonci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikhail_Dmitrievich_Tsitsianov.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pavel Dmitriyevich Tsitsianov was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 19 September 1754 into the Tsitsishvili, a Georgian noble family related to the Georgian royal dynasty. His grandfather had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the early 1700s among the émigrés accompanying the exiled King Vakhtang VI, and his father, Dmitry Pavlovich, was a writer and state official. Tsitsianov entered the Preobrazhensky Regiment in 1772, fought in the Russo-Turkish War of 1787–92 at Khotin, Ismail and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bender-moldova">Bender</a>, served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> during the 1794 uprising and joined the Persian Expedition of 1796 under Count Valerian Zubov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1802 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> made him commander of Russian forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, newly annexed, inspector of the Caucasian Line and military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, in effect giving him absolute authority over Russian forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. He exiled the remaining members of Georgia's former ruling dynasty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, upgraded the Georgian Military Road, kept Georgian as a local official language and founded a school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> for the sons of nobles. His letters to Caucasian rulers were full of threats and insults, and he held all those he called Asiatics in contempt.</p><p>His capture of Ganja in 1804, which he renamed Yelizavetpol, began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1804–1813. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/javad-khan">Javad Khan</a>, his son and between 1,500 and 3,000 inhabitants were killed, among them 500 warriors sheltering in a mosque. Tsitsianov then fought the Persian army at Gyumri, Etchmiadzin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yerevan">Yerevan</a>, and in May 1805 brought the Karabakh Khanate under Russian suzerainty by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kurekchay">Treaty of Kurekchay</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-khalil-khan">Ibrahim Khalil Khan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> and Shoragel followed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In February 1806 he rode up to the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> with only his aide-de-camp Prince Eristov and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> to receive the keys of the city, and was shot by the khan's men. His head and hands were cut off and, by some accounts, sent to Fath-Ali Shah in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. His body lay in a ditch until Russian forces took Baku months later, and his remains were later reinterred in the Tbilisi Sioni Cathedral.</p><p>Russian writers praised him, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin">Alexander Pushkin</a> among them, while the Iranian chronicler Reza-Qoli Khan Hedayat called him the shedder of blood. Muriel Atkin judged him undeserving of the praise, and Walter Richmond holds that he set in motion the brutality of later Russian efforts to conquer the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pavel Tsitsianov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1806 the Russian commander <b>Pavel Tsitsianov</b> was killed at the gates of Baku while accepting its surrender from Huseyngulu Khan.</blockquote><a href="/state/baku-khanate">Baku Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>In January 1804 Russian troops under <b>Pavel Tsitsianov</b> stormed Ganja, and Javad Khan was killed with his son.</blockquote><a href="/state/ganja-khanate">Ganja Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Baku, in what is now <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pavel Tsitsianov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
